# Titan's ingress path, owned by Flux. Issue #209; the reasoning is in
# docs/superpowers/specs/2026-10-31-titan-ingress-ownership-decision.md and the runbook in
# docs/superpowers/plans/2026-10-31-traefik-cutover-209.md. Read those before editing this file —
# several values here look arbitrary and are not.
#
# This is PHASE A: it installs a second Traefik that receives no traffic. The old k3s-bundled one is
# still the live path, still owns the `traefik` IngressClass, and still answers on 80/443. Nothing
# here is user-visible, which is exactly why it is the first PR to land. Phase B (the externalIPs and
# IngressClass handover) is a separate change with its own rollback, because the moment the address
# moves is the moment this becomes load-bearing.
#
# WHY NOT kube-system. k3s's bundled HelmChart already owns a Helm release named `traefik` there,
# and two owners for one release name is a mess no later commit cleans up. `traefik-system` also
# matches the sibling box, which is the precedent this whole design rests on.
#
# WHY THIS PIN. Chart 41.1.1 is the floor, not a preference: the parity pin (upstream 40.1.0, to
# match k3s's bundled 40.1.4+up40.1.0) cannot render at all, because 40.1.0 ships a
# proxy-max-version guard that rejects Traefik 3.7.8 as a hard error. Rancher's repackaging is the
# only reason that pairing works on the bundled install. The proxy itself stays bit-identical to
# today (rancher/mirrored-library-traefik:3.7.8) because the cutover already changes five things —
# controller ownership, exposure mechanism, externalTrafficPolicy, dashboard state, namespace — and
# a sixth simultaneous change means a regression cannot be attributed to plumbing or to Traefik.
# The 3.7.8 -> 3.7.16 bump is its own later PR, after this is proven.
#
# BLAST RADIUS, said out loud because it is severe. apply/00-bootstrap/stage-infra.yaml runs
# `wait: true` with `timeout: 10m0s`, stage-certificates dependsOn infra, and stage-apps dependsOn
# certificates. A Traefik HelmRelease that will not go Ready therefore blocks certificate renewals,
# every app, the whole tesla stage, and the restic backups. cert-manager's own comment states this
# cost; Traefik inherits it exactly.
# The Namespace lives HERE, not in apply/00-bootstrap/namespaces.yaml, and that makes it the second
# exception to the central-declaration rule after k8s-reader. Two reasons, both load-bearing:
#
# A HelmRelease and its HelmRepository must live in the namespace the release installs into, so the
# namespace and this release are one object graph, not a dependency between stages.
#
# And `make dry-run` would otherwise be unable to validate this stage at all. dry-run-check.sh runs
# `apply --dry-run=server` one stage at a time, and server-side dry-run does not persist anything -
# so a Namespace declared in 00-bootstrap is not there when 20-infra is asked. Declaring it centrally
# here produced `Error from server (NotFound): namespaces "traefik-system" not found` and a red gate.
# Keeping it in the stage that first uses it is what makes the stage self-contained enough to check.
apiVersion: v1
kind: Namespace
metadata:
  name: traefik-system
  labels:
    name: traefik-system
    pod-security.kubernetes.io/warn: restricted
    pod-security.kubernetes.io/warn-version: latest
  annotations:
    kubernetes.io/metadata.name: traefik-system
    kubernetes.io/description: "Flux-managed Traefik, the cluster's ingress path (issue #209)"
---
apiVersion: source.toolkit.fluxcd.io/v1
kind: HelmRepository
metadata:
  name: traefik
  namespace: traefik-system
spec:
  interval: 1h
  url: https://traefik.github.io/charts
---
apiVersion: helm.toolkit.fluxcd.io/v2
kind: HelmRelease
metadata:
  name: traefik
  namespace: traefik-system
spec:
  # Named explicitly, not left to Flux's default. The IngressClass handover in C3 writes
  # meta.helm.sh/release-name and release-namespace onto the live object, and those strings must
  # match what the release actually is or the adoption silently points at a release that does not
  # exist. Flux's default would produce `traefik`, but a handover that hard-codes an annotation must
  # not depend on a default it never names.
  releaseName: traefik
  chart:
    spec:
      chart: traefik
      version: "41.1.1"
      sourceRef:
        kind: HelmRepository
        name: traefik
  interval: 30m
  driftDetection:
    mode: enabled
  install:
    # `Create`, NOT cert-manager's `CreateReplace`. The chart ships its 25 CRDs in crds/, and
    # CreateReplace would replace all of them as a side effect of any values upgrade — mid-cutover,
    # on the objects every Middleware and IngressRouteTCP in this cluster depends on. CRD refresh is
    # an explicit, separately-reviewed kubectl apply instead (plan step C6).
    crds: Create
    strategy:
      name: RetryOnFailure
      retryInterval: 5m
  upgrade:
    crds: Create
    strategy:
      name: RetryOnFailure
      retryInterval: 5m
  values:
    image:
      registry: docker.io
      repository: rancher/mirrored-library-traefik
      tag: "3.7.8"

    # Phase A: the old chart still owns the IngressClass. Claiming it here fails the install with
    # AlreadyExists, and uninstalling the old chart first leaves every ingressClassName: traefik
    # Ingress unserved in the gap. C3 hands it over instead. `name: traefik` is set so that when
    # phase B flips enabled to true the object it creates is the one the Ingresses already name.
    ingressClass:
      # PHASE B. Flipping this to true is what makes the new Traefik route anything: Traefik resolves
      # the class filter from IngressClass objects whose spec.controller is
      # traefik.io/ingress-controller — 41.1.1 never emits an --providers.kubernetesingress.
      # ingress-class argument at all, so there is no flag to grep and no arg to set.
      #
      # HARD PRECONDITION, and it is the same class of failure that stalled the cluster on 2026-11-02.
      # IngressClass/traefik already exists and is owned by helm release `traefik` in namespace
      # `kube-system` (verified: meta.helm.sh/release-name=traefik, release-namespace=kube-system).
      # If this merges before that ownership is handed over, helm refuses with `IngressClass "traefik"
      # already exists`, the HelmRelease goes InstallFailed, stage-infra waits, and certificates, apps,
      # tesla, databases and backup all drop to DependencyNotReady. Hand the ownership over FIRST:
      #   kubectl -n kube-system annotate helmcharts.helm.cattle.io traefik \
      #     helmcharts.helm.cattle.io/unmanaged=true --overwrite
      #   kubectl -n kube-system patch helmcharts.helm.cattle.io traefik -p '{"metadata":{"finalizers":null}}'
      #   kubectl patch ingressclass traefik -p '{"metadata":{"annotations":{"meta.helm.sh/release-name":"traefik","meta.helm.sh/release-namespace":"traefik-system"}}}'
      # The last line is the adoption; the first two are what stop the old release from taking the CRDs
      # with it. Do not reorder them — annotating before stripping the finalizer is what makes the
      # strip safe.
      enabled: true
      isDefaultClass: true
      name: traefik

    # STRICTER than today, not equivalent. The live kube-system/traefik Deployment carries
    # --api.dashboard=true and --ping=true, so the dashboard is ENABLED and merely unreachable
    # (:8080 is not in the Service and no IngressRoute exists). Do not describe this line as matching
    # current behaviour; it does not. --ping=true is not a values key at all — the chart hardcodes
    # it and the probes use it — so it stays either way.
    api:
      dashboard: false

    ports:
      websecure:
        http:
          tls:
            # Already the chart default, set explicitly because today's Deployment really does carry
            # --entryPoints.websecure.http.tls=true and this must not depend on a default surviving
            # a chart bump. investing-agent and llm pin router.entrypoints: websecure; without TLS
            # termination there the dashboard would answer on cleartext.
            enabled: true

    service:
      # Deliberately NO helm.sh/resource-policy: keep. On a cloud LoadBalancer that annotation earns
      # its place by preserving an IP the provider allocated. There is no allocated IP: the externalIP
      # is the node's own statically-configured address, and it survives whatever happens to this
      # object. What keep WOULD do is worse than useless — a kept Service carrying externalIPs
      # outlives the release that made it, so a later uninstall leaves the public address DNAT-ing to
      # endpoints that no longer exist. With externalTrafficPolicy: Local and zero local endpoints,
      # kube-proxy has nowhere to send the packet: the host stops answering on 80/443 while
      # `kubectl get svc` still shows the address and nothing owns it. Letting Helm delete the
      # Service on uninstall is correct, and it is the default.
      #
      # Everything goes under spec. service.spec is a verbatim passthrough (templates/_service.tpl
      # renders toYaml .service.spec plus the chart's own selector). The OLD top-level service.type
      # is NOT read by 41.x — it is silently ignored and the chart default renders instead, with no
      # error. Chart PR #1686 moved type, externalIPs and ipFamilyPolicy here.
      spec:
        # Phase A: ClusterIP with no externalIPs, so nothing reaches this Traefik from outside.
        # Phase B adds externalIPs: [<TITAN_PUBLIC_IP>] — the node's own address, which is why no
        # LoadBalancer controller and no MetalLB is needed at all.
        # The address is committed in the clear on purpose, and it is a deliberate exception to the
        # repo's "no concrete public IPv4" rule, allow-listed in leak-check with this file as its
        # citation. It is not a secret: it is the address every client dials, it is in DNS, and the
        # manifest cannot work with a placeholder. Hiding it in a sops Secret would buy no
        # confidentiality and would add a real failure mode — a mis-named key or an empty list leaves
        # the release Ready with nothing answering on 80/443. tools/traefik-exposure-check.py is what
        # makes that shape fail loudly instead.
        externalIPs:
          - "37.187.159.156"
        # Paired with externalIPs, in this change, deliberately. #214 exists because these two were
        # split across phases: externalTrafficPolicy may only be set on an externally-accessible
        # Service, and the Service only becomes externally accessible when externalIPs appears.
        # Setting it here is legal for the first time. Chosen, not inherited — both titan today and
        # the sibling box run Cluster, which SNATs, so Traefik has never seen a real client source IP
        # here; Local restores it, and on a single node it is safe. The footgun is real and written
        # down: the day a second node appears or this pod is unschedulable, Local drops all traffic
        # instead of load-balancing.
        type: ClusterIP
        # externalTrafficPolicy is DELIBERATELY ABSENT in phase A, and it is not an oversight.
        #
        # The API server refuses it: `spec.externalTrafficPolicy: Invalid value: "Local": may only be
        # set for externally-accessible services`. Measured on the live cluster 2026-11-02 — this
        # value was merged in #211 and the HelmRelease went InstallFailed, which stalled stage-infra
        # and with it certificates, apps, tesla, databases and backup, because stage-infra waits.
        # "Externally accessible" means NodePort, LoadBalancer, or a non-empty externalIPs. A phase A
        # Service is a bare ClusterIP with no externalIPs, so it is not externally accessible and the
        # field is not merely useless here, it is illegal.
        #
        # Nothing is lost by omitting it: externalTrafficPolicy only governs traffic arriving through
        # externalIPs/NodePort/LoadBalancer, and in phase A no such traffic exists.
        #
        # It belongs in phase B, set in the SAME change that adds externalIPs — the two are a pair,
        # and splitting them is what produced this failure. Chosen there, not inherited: both titan
        # today and the sibling box run Cluster, which SNATs, so Traefik has never seen a real client
        # source IP here. On a single node Local is safe and restores it; that is half the reason this
        # exposure mechanism was picked. The footgun is written down there too: the day a second node
        # appears or this pod is unschedulable, Local drops all traffic instead of load-balancing.
        ipFamilyPolicy: PreferDualStack

    providers:
      kubernetesIngress:
        publishedService:
          # Phase A: with no externalIPs, publishing would copy this Service's ClusterIP into every
          # Ingress .status while the old Traefik is still the real path. Turn it on in C4 together
          # with externalIPs, so the status only ever moves to an address that actually answers.
          # PHASE B, in the same change as externalIPs. With externalIPs set, publishing copies the
        # public address into every Ingress .status — which is the point. Turning it on before the
        # address exists would copy this Service's ClusterIP into every Ingress while the old Traefik
        # is still the real path, i.e. rewrite every status to an address that does not answer.
        enabled: true

    priorityClassName: system-cluster-critical
    tolerations:
      - key: "CriticalAddonsOnly"
        operator: "Exists"
      - key: "node-role.kubernetes.io/control-plane"
        operator: "Exists"
        effect: "NoSchedule"

    accessLog:
      # Top-level accessLog, NOT logs.accessLog — that block is 40.x and 41.x rejects it at render
      # time under the chart's root-strict values.schema.json. Enabled because it is the only way to
      # prove the client source IP actually survives, which is the point of externalTrafficPolicy.
      enabled: true

    # Deliberately NOT set, with the reason:
    #   deployment.podAnnotations prometheus.io/*  — k3s sets these because Rancher bundles a
    #     Prometheus. titan runs none; casa scrapes by target over the mesh. Inert config is noise,
    #     not parity.
    #   providers.kubernetesCRD.allowCrossNamespace / crossProviderNamespaces — no middleware
    #     reference in this tree crosses a namespace boundary (plan step P2c proves it per-object).
    #   ports.web.http.redirections.entryPoint — there is no HTTP->HTTPS redirect today and the chart
    #     adds none by default. Adding one is a behaviour change nobody asked for.
    #   tlsStore / defaultCertificate — every Ingress carries spec.tls with a Secret in its own
    #     namespace, and today's install has no default certificate either. If one is ever wanted it
    #     means adding traefik-system to the secretTemplate allow-list in
    #     apply/40-certificates/titan-wildcard.yaml, never copying the Secret by hand.
